# AMD64 thunk export spec -- dwrite.dll.
#
# From the 2026-08-17 everyday-DLL pass.  Anything built against a modern
# Windows UI stack imports dwrite -- launchers, overlays, in-game browsers --
# and this module had NO guest thunk at all: the loader found the native ppc64
# builtin, correctly refused to splice it into a guest call, and the import
# reported c000007b BEFORE the process ran a single instruction.  The export
# list is this module's own .spec, which names exactly one entry point.
DLL dwrite.dll
FROM-SPEC auto

# dwrite.h is generated from dwrite.idl and is not in the global probe
# (wine_sig.PROBE_SRC).  dwrite_3.h is the umbrella -- it includes dwrite_2.h,
# which includes dwrite_1.h, which includes dwrite.h -- so one line declares the
# whole family and DWRITE_FACTORY_TYPE with it.  Measured: 0 of 1 before, 1 of 1
# after.
PROBE-EXTRA   dwrite_3.h

# ------------------------------------------------------------ the refusal
# THE ONE EXPORT VENDS AN INTERFACE, SO IT IS NOT A PASS-THROUGH.
# DWriteCreateFactory writes an IDWriteFactory* through its third argument.
# This module publishes no COM roster, so that pointer would reach the guest
# as a NATIVE ppc64 vtable and the guest's first method call on it would
# execute ppc64 bytes as x86-64.
#
# GUEST-IMPL points the GUEST's export at a wrapper in the native module
# (dlls/dwrite/guestthunk.c) that answers E_NOTIMPL and says why, by name, in
# an ERR.  The plain DWriteCreateFactory export is untouched and still serves
# native ppc64 callers -- gdi32 and d2d1 both use it -- which is the whole
# reason the redirect is on the guest side only.
#
# WHY THIS IS WORTH DOING AT ALL WHEN THE ONE EXPORT IS REFUSED.  Without a
# .thunks file the module has no guest form, and a guest that merely LISTS
# dwrite.dll in its import table dies at load with c000007b having run nothing.
# With it the module loads, the process starts, and the failure -- if it comes
# at all -- arrives at the call, as a documented HRESULT the caller can handle.
# That is the same distinction dlls/dbghelp/dbghelp.thunks was written for.
#
# WHAT WOULD LIFT IT: a COM-JSON roster for the DirectWrite surface plus a
# winecom instance in this module, exactly as dlls/dinput8 does in this same
# series.  It is a large roster -- IDWriteFactory alone is 26 slots and it
# vends IDWriteTextFormat, IDWriteTextLayout, IDWriteFontCollection,
# IDWriteFontFace and IDWriteGdiInterop, each of which vends more -- and two of
# its argument shapes have no winecom class yet: IDWriteTextRenderer and
# IDWriteFontCollectionLoader are objects the APPLICATION implements and hands
# to native code, which is the reverse-proxy direction (design §6, step 5).
GUEST-IMPL DWriteCreateFactory  __wine_guest_DWriteCreateFactory

# Internal name, kept out of the guest export list entirely: the guest never
# imports it, it is only the native symbol the redirect above resolves to.
EXCLUDE    __wine_guest_DWriteCreateFactory
